emu68 Driver Stack @PACKAGE_VERSION@
========================
@BUILD_NOTICE@AmigaOS drivers for Raspberry Pi 4 / Compute Module 4 running Emu68.

*** UPGRADING FROM 2.0? Two things can change how your machine comes up.
*** Network interfaces are no longer configured in ENVARC:netstack.prefs -
*** each one now has its own file in DEVS:NetInterfaces/ - so a fixed address
*** set up under 2.0 comes back up on DHCP until you re-enter it there; the
*** Install script warns you when it sees an old prefs file. And NVMe
*** partition numbering can shift, because exFAT partitions now mount too.
*** Keep a bootable backup of LIBS: and DEVS: before installing.


THIS ARCHIVE
------------

  Debug output: @DEBUG_BACKEND@
  @DEBUG_BACKEND_DESCRIPTION@

  @CACHE_OPS_DESCRIPTION@


WHAT TO INSTALL
---------------

The one thing that decides everything: does your Emu68 have the dcache
extensions? Official Emu68 releases up to 1.1.0-beta.1 do not have them - they
came to its JIT later, so having them means running a newer Emu68 or a custom
build:

  https://github.com/rondoval/Emu68/releases/tag/v1.1-alpha-with-rangeops

Without them the drivers still work, but the cache housekeeping around every
transfer has to be done the slow way - and the bundled TCP/IP stack, which is
built around that housekeeping being cheap, ends up SLOWER than the plain old
SANA-II driver. So on those releases there is no point installing the netdev
Ethernet driver at all: take the standard archive and pair the SANA-II driver
with Roadshow, AmiTCP or Miami. With the extensions, take the '-rangeops'
archive and the netdev driver with the bundled lwip-amiga stack, which gets you
close to gigabit line rate. USB is a separate, independent question: it depends
only on which USB stack you run.

The VideoCore parts (video, JPEG pictures, sound) need an Emu68 newer than
1.1.0-beta.1, which has the extensions too, so for those take the '-rangeops'
archive.

The installer asks two separate questions - which genet.device, and which
TCP/IP stack. They are independent now: since its 1.5 release the bundled
lwip-amiga stack drives classic SANA-II Ethernet drivers as well as the
netdev one, so the SANA-II genet works with either stack.

Find your row:

  Emu68            USB stack     Archive             Ethernet drv  TCP/IP     USB
  ---------------  ------------  ------------------  ------------  ---------  ---
  without dcache   Poseidon 4.x  <ver>.lha           SANA-II 3.x   any        5.x
  without dcache   Neptune 6.x   <ver>.lha           SANA-II 3.x   any        6.x
  with dcache      Poseidon 4.x  <ver>-rangeops.lha  netdev 4.x    bundled    5.x
  with dcache      Neptune 6.x   <ver>-rangeops.lha  netdev 4.x    bundled    6.x

  without dcache   official Emu68 releases, 1.1 alpha.1 to 1.1.0-beta.1
  with dcache      an Emu68 newer than 1.1.0-beta.1, or a custom build
  Poseidon 4.x     classic Poseidon (Chris Hodges)
  Neptune 6.x      Neptune, an incompatible fork of the Poseidon USB stack
                   (6.0-6.2 were published as "Poseidon for AmigaOS")
  <ver>.lha        emu68-drivers-@PACKAGE_VERSION@.lha
  any              your existing Roadshow/AmiTCP/Miami, or the bundled stack

INSTALLING THE BUNDLED STACK REPLACES YOUR bsdsocket.library, so Roadshow,
AmiTCP and Miami stop working. It also installs its own ping, traceroute, arp,
AddNetInterface, RemoveNetInterface, NetShutdown, GetNetStatus and
ShowNetStatus into C:, over the Roadshow commands of those names. THERE IS NO
UNDO - going back means reinstalling your old stack. Your SANA-II
Ethernet HARDWARE keeps working - the bundled stack drives it, you just
describe it in a DEVS:NetInterfaces/ file instead. What it cannot drive is
non-Ethernet SANA-II: PPP and SLIP dial-up, Token Ring, ArcNet. Note also that
the stack carries ONE interface at a time besides loopback, so it is the Pi's
port or your card, not both.

xhci.device (6.x) speaks only Neptune's own context interface, so it does not
work with Poseidon. xhci-legacy.device (5.x) speaks the classic interface and
runs on both, on Neptune just without real USB 3.0 or fast UAS storage - so it
is the safe answer if you are unsure. The two have different names and can
both be installed.

The two '-serial' archives are the same drivers with serial-port diagnostics -
take one only when chasing a problem. Installing a '-rangeops' archive on an
Emu68 without the extensions is safe: the Install script checks, says so, and
stops without changing anything.

Where the advice comes from - measured TCP throughput over gigabit Ethernet,
using genet.device with the bundled lwip-amiga stack:

  Emu68 without extensions,   standard archive   104 Mbit/s in,  79 out
  Emu68 with the extensions,  standard archive   698 Mbit/s in, 477 out
  Emu68 with the extensions,  -rangeops archive  near line rate

Those are the netdev driver. Over the SANA-II backend the same stack measures
about 290 Mbit/s in and 300 out: SANA-II copies every packet and cannot offload
checksums, so it gives up most of the netdev gain.


COMPONENTS
----------

gic400.library @GIC400_VERSION@ (LIBS:)
  The interrupt controller library every driver here needs, including
  bcmpcie.library. Installed like any other library in the stack. If your
  Emu68 carries its own copy, that one is already in memory at boot and the
  drivers use it, so the LIBS: file is simply never opened - harmless, and it
  keeps things working on an Emu68 without the library.

bcmpcie.library @BCMPCIE_VERSION@ (LIBS:)
  Talks to the Pi's PCIe bus: brings the link up, finds the devices on it and
  makes them available to the drivers.

xhci.device, xhci-legacy.device (DEVS:USBHardware/)
  USB 2.0/3.0 for the Raspberry Pi 4 / CM4. Every USB controller it finds is a
  separate UNIT, and each unit has to be added to your USB stack on its own -
  which units exist depends on your board. See the driver's own README in
  Documentation/emu68-xhci-driver-context/ (or .../-legacy/ for the 5.x
  version) for the unit numbering.
  The driver comes in two versions; the installer asks which (see WHAT TO
  INSTALL above), and you can have both. The USB stack itself is NOT part of
  this archive.

    xhci.device @XHCI_VERSION@  for Neptune 6.2 or newer. Shows USB 3.0 devices as
      what they are instead of disguising them as USB 2.0, and supports the
      fast transfer mode UAS mass storage uses.

    xhci-legacy.device @XHCI_LEGACY_VERSION@  required on classic Poseidon
      4.x, and it also runs on Neptune - but there SuperSpeed devices appear
      as USB 2.0 and the fast storage mode is not available. Up to 5.5 this
      driver was called xhci.device; to use the new one, select
      xhci-legacy.device in Trident (see AFTER INSTALLATION).

genet.device (DEVS:Networks/)
  Gigabit Ethernet for the port built into the Raspberry Pi 4 / CM4. It comes
  in two versions and you pick ONE at install time (see WHAT TO INSTALL above);
  the choice no longer decides your TCP/IP stack:

    netdev  @GENET_VERSION@   the fast zero-copy driver. Only the bundled
      lwip-amiga stack can open it, so Roadshow, AmiTCP and Miami cannot -
      choosing it installs the bundled stack as well. Its netdev-stats
      counter tool installs to C:.

    SANA-II @GENET_SANA2_VERSION@  the classic driver. Works with a third-party
      stack (Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami) AND with the bundled one, which has
      driven Ethernet SANA-II devices since lwip-amiga 1.5 - slower than
      netdev either way. It rides in the archive under Storage/ and is
      installed to DEVS:Networks/ only if you choose it; its genet-stats
      counter tool then installs to C:.

lwip-amiga @LWIP_VERSION@ (LIBS:bsdsocket.library @BSDSOCKET_VERSION@)
  A modern TCP/IP stack. It provides the standard AmigaOS networking API under
  the usual library name, which is why installing it replaces whatever
  bsdsocket.library you have.
  Opening it starts the stack (loopback only); the network interface is added
  at boot by S:Network-Startup running 'AddNetInterface', from the config file
  DEVS:NetInterfaces/genet (DHCP by default; the file holds a fixed address
  or VLAN too). Stack-wide settings - hostname, DNS servers, mDNS - live in
  ENVARC:netstack.prefs.
  Installing it REPLACES any existing bsdsocket.library, and its commands
  below replace the Roadshow ones of the same name in C:. Neither is undone
  by re-running the installer - reinstall your old stack to go back.
  Besides the netdev genet it also drives classic Ethernet-type SANA-II
  drivers - a Zorro or PCMCIA card, a USB Ethernet adapter - which it detects
  automatically; write a file for one by copying the commented sample from
  SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces/genet and editing DEVICE=. Interface files from
  Roadshow work unchanged, but Roadshow keeps a fixed address's router and
  DNS servers in DEVS:Internet, which this stack does not read: add GATEWAY=
  to the interface file and DNS1=/DNS2= to ENVARC:netstack.prefs. DHCP needs
  neither. Non-Ethernet SANA-II (PPP, SLIP, Token Ring, ArcNet) is not
  supported.
  ONE interface at a time: besides loopback the stack carries a single
  interface, so a second AddNetInterface is refused. If DEVS:NetInterfaces/
  holds more than one file the boot line picks the highest PRI= tooltype and
  silently skips the rest - keep the spares in SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces/.

netdev-stats (C:)
  Installed with the netdev genet.device: shows that driver's own counters and
  interface statistics live. It speaks the netdev command set directly, so it
  does not work against the SANA-II driver - that one has genet-stats.

netinfo, mdns, GetNetStatus, ShowNetStatus (C:)
  Networking tools installed with the bundled stack. netinfo reports the
  address and DNS settings the stack ended up with; mdns looks up and
  announces .local names; GetNetStatus and ShowNetStatus are the
  Roadshow-compatible status query and report.

AddNetInterface, RemoveNetInterface, NetShutdown, arp (C:)
  Roadshow-style network control, installed with the bundled stack.
  AddNetInterface adds interfaces from DEVS:NetInterfaces/ files (blocking
  until the DHCP lease arrives); RemoveNetInterface takes one down again
  (FORCE overrides the in-use refusal); NetShutdown stops the whole stack
  and unloads the library once every network program has quit; arp displays,
  sets and deletes ARP table entries (published/proxy entries are not
  supported).

ping, traceroute (C:)
  The classic 4.4BSD network diagnostics, installed with the bundled stack,
  with Roadshow-compatible command templates. ping sends ICMP echo requests
  and reports round-trip times and packet loss; traceroute maps the gateways
  a packet crosses on its way to a host (ping's RECORDROUTE option is not
  supported by this stack).

NetLogViewer (C:, with icon)
  Log viewer, installed with the bundled stack: a Commodity
  that captures every message the stack and its clients log
  (interface and link state, DHCP leases, errors, syslog() output) and shows
  them in a window with time, origin and severity; the list can be saved to
  a file. Shift-Alt-F8 (CX_POPKEY) shows the window, Exchange hides and
  shows it, NetShutdown makes it exit. Start it before AddNetInterface to
  see the whole bring-up, for example from S:Network-Startup with
  "Run >NIL: C:NetLogViewer CX_POPUP NO". The icon carries the CX_POPKEY /
  CX_PRIORITY / CX_POPUP tooltypes and DONOTWAIT, so a copy in WBStartup
  works too.

NetSpeed (C:, with icon)
  Throughput monitor, installed with the bundled stack: a window showing
  current, average and peak speed in both directions, the link speed and a
  graph of the last few minutes, for any interface. Everything is set from
  the Settings menu, and Settings > Save keeps it in ENV:NetSpeed.prefs. The
  window iconifies to an AppIcon; NetShutdown closes it. Start it from
  Workbench, or with "Run >NIL: C:NetSpeed".

nvme.device @NVME_VERSION@ (DEVS:)
  Storage driver for a PCIe-attached NVMe SSD.

nvmeinfo, nvmeadm (C:)
  Tools for nvme.device, installed automatically with the driver. nvmeinfo
  lists the drives and their namespaces; nvmeadm reports SMART/health data,
  log pages, self-test results and firmware information.

vchiq.device, videocodec.library @VCHIQ_VERSION@ (DEVS:, LIBS:) [experimental]
  Hardware video and pictures through the Pi's VideoCore chip: it decodes
  H.264 and MJPEG video and JPEG, PNG, GIF, BMP, TGA and PPM pictures,
  encodes H.264 and JPEG, and scales and converts pictures, while the 68k
  stays almost idle. Programs open videocodec.library, which drives the VideoCore through
  its codec driver LIBS:videocodec/videocore.codec; that one reaches the
  firmware through vchiq.device. It needs settings in the Pi's config.txt
  (see AFTER INSTALLATION), and the installer leaves it unticked. What works
  and what does not yet: Documentation/emu68-vchiq-library/README.md.

VCGrab (SYS:Tools/Commodities)
  Installed with videocodec.library. A commodity that records an RTG screen
  to an MKV file on hot keys and grabs stills, the VideoCore doing the work.
  Double-click its icon for the settings window; a copy in WBStartup starts
  it at boot. From the shell, with its full name, it records one file or
  grabs one still. No sound or mouse pointer:
  Documentation/emu68-vchiq-library/README.md.

VCInfo (C:)
  Installed with videocodec.library. VCInfo checks the setup and says what
  to fix ('VCInfo'), and lists what the hardware can do ('VCInfo CODECS').

videocore.audio @VCHIQ_VERSION@ (DEVS:AHI, DEVS:AudioModes) [experimental]
  An AHI driver: every AHI program plays through the VideoCore to HDMI0,
  HDMI1 or the Pi's headphone jack, at 8000 to 48000 Hz: three modes
  (16-bit, HiFi, and 7.1 for an AV receiver on HDMI with AHI 6), the
  output chosen per unit in the AHI preferences. It needs AHI and
  vchiq.device, which the installer brings along with VCInfo, and only the
  cma overlay, not gpu_mem. Its latency is about 40 ms; it does not record.

vcjpeg.datatype @VCHIQ_VERSION@ (SYS:Classes/DataTypes) [experimental]
  JPEG pictures through the VideoCore, for every program that loads
  pictures through datatypes (MultiView, Workbench, browsers). It takes
  baseline JPEGs of about 2 megapixels and more (twice as fast as software
  from 5 megapixels); smaller, progressive and CMYK ones stay with your
  JPEG datatype, as does any picture the VideoCore cannot decode. Its
  descriptor (SYS:Storage/DataTypes/VCJPEG) is added from S:User-Startup,
  after the system's: remove that line to go back. It brings videocodec
  along.

openpci.library @OPENPCI_VERSION@ (LIBS:)
  The openpci API for programs that list PCI devices through it, such as
  identify.library; it forwards those calls to bcmpcie.library. Only one
  openpci.library can be installed: if another one is there - most likely a
  real PCI expansion board's (Mediator, Prometheus, G-REX, ...) - the
  installer leaves it unticked and asks before replacing it, since that
  board's software would stop working.

lspci (C:)
  Lists the devices found on the PCIe bus. Run it from a Shell to check the
  link is up and everything was detected. Installed with bcmpcie.library; if
  another lspci is in C: - most likely a PCI board's - the installer asks
  before replacing it.

Developer/ (in the archive, never installed)
  For programmers: the headers of the libraries and devices here (include),
  gic400.library's autodoc and FD file (Autodocs, FD), a minimal video player
  and picture viewer with their sources (Examples/videocodec), and the test
  tools VCTest and VPUPlay (Tools).


INSTALLATION
------------

Run the included 'Install' script using the Commodore Installer tool
(v43.3 or later, included with AmigaOS 3.1.4+).

The first screen names the archive you are installing from. The installer
then asks which USB drivers you want (xhci.device, xhci-legacy.device, both
or none - ticked as the machine has them), ONE Ethernet driver (netdev genet,
SANA-II genet, or none) and - unless you picked netdev, which answers it for
you - which TCP/IP stack (the bundled lwip-amiga, or leave bsdsocket.library
alone). The Ethernet and TCP/IP answers start at what you have installed; on
a machine with no genet.device the standard archive suggests the SANA-II
driver and the -rangeops archive netdev, as the table above recommends. On a
Poseidon 4.x machine whose xhci.device is still the 5.x driver, installing the
6.x xhci.device replaces it; the installer asks first.

Then pick the other components from a checklist (videocodec, the VideoCore
video support, videocore.audio, its sound, and vcjpeg.datatype, its
pictures, are experimental and unticked). Tools come with what they serve:
nvmeinfo and nvmeadm with nvme.device, lspci with bcmpcie.library. The
installer pulls in the libraries the drivers need automatically, so you
cannot end up with a driver whose library is missing; in Expert mode the
checklist also offers gic400.library and bcmpcie.library on their own. The
VideoCore parts start ticked once they are installed, so an upgrade replaces
them. The last screen before anything is copied lists what will be installed;
Abort there leaves your system as it was.

In Novice mode the installer asks only before replacing something it should
not replace unasked: it keeps what the machine has, takes the default answers
above, and shows the result on that same last screen.

When the bundled stack is to replace another one, the installer asks first.
Answer 'Keep my stack' and your bsdsocket.library and commands stay; if you
had picked netdev, which only the bundled stack can open, you get the SANA-II
driver instead. Updating the bundled stack does not ask.

A copy already on your system is left alone when it is the same file, and
replaced when it is the same version from another archive (switching between
the standard, -rangeops and -serial archives replaces every driver). Only a
newer copy than the one in the archive makes the installer ask before going
back. The chosen genet.device is always replaced, since both drivers share
the name, and so is bsdsocket.library when you choose the bundled stack.

If you are upgrading from 2.0 the installer shows one extra screen, telling
you that the interface settings in your ENVARC:netstack.prefs are no longer
read. It changes nothing - see AFTER INSTALLATION for what to move where.

On the -rangeops archives the Install script first checks that your Emu68 has
the dcache extensions and refuses to continue if it does not. If you are
preparing a disk on a machine that is not the target, it warns and lets you
carry on. On the standard archive the same check tells you when your Emu68
has the extensions, so the -rangeops archive would suit it better. The small
tool it uses for its checks runs from the unpacked archive and is never copied
to C:.

Manual installation: copy files to the destinations listed above and reboot.


AFTER INSTALLATION
------------------

Reboot, then finish the steps the drivers cannot do for themselves. The
installer's closing screen lists the ones that apply to your selection; the
full set is:

  xhci.device
             Install Neptune 6.x (distributed separately), then add EACH
             xhci.device unit as a USB host controller in Neptune USB,
             or from a Shell with:
               USBAddHardware xhci.device <unit>
             (AddUSBHardware before Neptune 6.3). The driver's README in
             Documentation/ lists the units.

  xhci-legacy.device
             Install a USB stack, then add EACH xhci-legacy.device unit as
             a USB host controller in Trident (or Neptune USB). Poseidon set
             up with the old xhci.device (5.5 or older) keeps working on it;
             to use this version, change the device in Trident's Hardware
             page to xhci-legacy.device, save, and then delete
             DEVS:USBHardware/xhci.device - unless the installer already put
             the 6.x driver there for Neptune. On Neptune xhci.device is the
             faster one.

  bundled    S:Network-Startup adds the network interface at boot with
  stack      AddNetInterface (DHCP; per-interface settings live in
             DEVS:NetInterfaces/genet, stack-wide ones in
             ENVARC:netstack.prefs). netinfo and ShowNetStatus show what the
             stack ended up with; NetShutdown stops the network again.
             To use a Zorro/PCMCIA card or a USB Ethernet adapter instead of
             the Pi's port, copy SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces/genet into
             DEVS:NetInterfaces/ under a name of your choosing, set DEVICE=
             (and UNIT=) to your driver, and remove the genet file - only
             one interface can be up at a time.

  SANA-II    If you kept your own TCP/IP stack, point it (Roadshow, AmiTCP,
  + own      Miami) at 'genet.device' unit 0 before it can carry traffic.
  stack      genet-stats shows the driver's counters.

  netdev     netdev-stats shows the driver's own counters and loss points.

  upgrading  Interface settings from a 2.0 ENVARC:netstack.prefs are ignored
  from 2.0   now; move them into DEVS:NetInterfaces/genet, which the
             installer created for you:
               MODE=STATIC + ADDRESS/NETMASK/GATEWAY  ->  a dotted-quad
                 ADDRESS= plus NETMASK= (and GATEWAY= if you had one)
               DEVICE, UNIT, VLAN                     ->  same keys, same
                 values, just in the interface file
             DHCP setups need nothing - that is the default. HOSTNAME, DNS1,
             DNS2, MDNS and NETWORK stay in netstack.prefs and still work.

  nvme       nvmeinfo lists the NVMe drives and namespaces that were found;
             nvmeadm reports SMART/health data and log pages.

  videocodec Add dtoverlay=cma (the memory vchiq.device shares with the
             VideoCore) to config.txt on the Pi's boot partition, and
             gpu_mem=128 (memory for the VideoCore's codecs) for pictures
             over 24 megapixels, two videos at once and a video during a
             recording: the default of 76 plays one 1080p video or records
             a 1080p screen. Add dtoverlay=videocore,mem=32 for
             full-screen video at 24 or 32 bits. Emu68 reads config.txt
             only when the Pi starts, so then press Ctrl-Amiga-Amiga or
             power-cycle. 'VCInfo' then checks the setup, says what to fix,
             and ends with PASS.

  videocore.audio
             Reboot (or run 'AddAudioModes REFRESH'), then pick a
             "VideoCore" mode and its output (HDMI0, HDMI1, Headphone
             jack) for a unit in the AHI preferences. If an HDMI display
             stays silent, the Pi drives it as DVI: add hdmi_drive=2 to
             config.txt and press Ctrl-Amiga-Amiga.

  vcjpeg.datatype
             Reboot: the line in S:User-Startup adds the descriptor. It
             needs the videocodec settings above.

  gic400     Nothing to do. If your Emu68 supplies gic400.library from its own
             ROM, that copy is already in memory at boot and the drivers use
             it, so the LIBS: copy simply goes unused.


REQUIREMENTS
------------

  - Raspberry Pi 4 or CM4 running @CACHE_OPS_FIRMWARE_REQ@
  - AmigaOS 3.x
  - USB stack (for the xhci drivers), distributed separately. The 6.x driver
    needs Neptune 6.2 or newer (6.2 was published as "Poseidon for AmigaOS");
    the 5.x driver runs on classic Poseidon 4.x and on Neptune alike
  - Networking (for genet.device): the netdev version requires the bundled
    lwip-amiga stack; the SANA-II version works with either that or a
    third-party stack such as Roadshow, AmiTCP or Miami. The bundled stack
    needs no genet at all if you connect through an Ethernet-type SANA-II
    card or USB adapter
  - Video (videocodec.library): Emu68 newer than 1.1.0-beta.1 with the cma
    overlay, gpu_mem=128 for pictures over 24 megapixels, two videos at
    once and a video during a recording, and Picasso96 with an RTG screen for
    the players
  - Sound (videocore.audio): AHI 4 or 6, Emu68 newer than 1.1.0-beta.1 with
    the cma overlay
  - Pictures (vcjpeg.datatype): AmigaOS 3.2 (picture.datatype 47), and what
    videocodec.library needs


